\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $G$ be any group. A nontrivial finite left-coset partition is a family $\{g_iH_i:1\leq i\leq k\}$ with $k\geq2$, $g_i\in G$, and subgroups $H_i\leq G$ of finite index, such that the cosets are pairwise disjoint and their union is $G$. The index $[G:H_i]$ is the number of left cosets of $H_i$ in $G$. The subgroups themselves need not be distinct.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
In every such partition, must there exist distinct indices $i,j\in\{1,\ldots,k\}$ with
\[
[G:H_i]=[G:H_j]?
\]
Equivalently, can a nontrivial finite coset partition ever have all its subgroup indices pairwise distinct? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.03569}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must two subgroup indices repeat whenever finitely many nontrivial left cosets partition a group?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1310 (ALG-014). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Leo Margolis and Ofir Schnabel, \emph{The Herzog--Sch\"onheim Conjecture for small groups and harmonic subgroups}, arXiv:1803.03569v1 (2018), Section 1, displayed HSC. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.03569}.
\end{description}
